TREE(1)                       Funny CLI Manual                       TREE(1)

NAME
    tree — Display a directory as a tree, because a flat list was
           apparently too peaceful.

SYNOPSIS
    tree [path] [depth]

DESCRIPTION
    The 'tree' command recursively displays the contents of a directory
    using branches, connectors, and just enough visual structure to make
    your filesystem look like an actual civilization.

    Directories are shown with a trailing '/'.

    By default, the tree is displayed to a maximum depth of 3.

    Directories are listed before files, and both groups are sorted
    alphabetically without regard to case.

    Because chaos belongs in the filesystem, not in the output.

ARGUMENTS
    path
        The directory or filesystem entry to display.

        Optional. If omitted, the CLI workspace is used.

        In other words:

            tree

        means:

            "Show me what I've done to this place."

    depth
        Maximum recursion depth.

        Defaults to 3.

        Increase it if you're brave. Decrease it if your project has
        become an ecosystem.

OUTPUT
    The root entry is printed first without a connector.

    Child entries use familiar tree branches:

        ├── item
        └── item

    Nested entries are indented appropriately using vertical branch
    markers.

    Directories appear with '/':

        project/
        ├── src/
        │   └── main.py
        └── README.md

    Beautiful. Informative. Mildly judgmental.

SORTING
    Entries are ordered as follows:

        1. Directories
        2. Files and other non-directories

    Within each group, names are sorted alphabetically,
    case-insensitively.

    Because finding 'src/' buried between 47 unrelated files would
    simply be rude.

DEPTH
    The root is depth 0.

    Children are depth 1.

    Grandchildren are depth 2.

    And so on.

    Once the requested maximum depth is reached, the command stops
    descending.

    The tree knows when to quit.

EXAMPLES
    > tree
        Displays the workspace up to depth 3.

        A bird's-eye view of your filesystem empire.

    > tree src
        Displays the tree starting at 'src'.

        Because the entire project was getting a little overwhelming.

    > tree . 1
        Shows only the root and its immediate children.

        The filesystem equivalent of making eye contact and leaving.

    > tree project 5
        Goes five levels deep.

        At this point you are no longer browsing.
        You are exploring.

    > tree .
        Shows the current CLI workspace.

        "Let me just see what's in here."

        Famous last words.

FUN FACTS
    - Directories are displayed before files.
    - Sorting is case-insensitive.
    - Directories receive a trailing '/'.
    - The default depth is 3.
    - Tree branches use Unicode box-drawing characters.
    - The root gets special treatment and doesn't wear a connector.
    - The command recursively visits directories only until the
      requested depth is reached.
    - It does not hide dotfiles. If they're there, they're invited
      to the tree.
    - A sufficiently deep tree may reveal architectural decisions
      you'd rather not discuss.

